Use a reverse priority comparer:
new PriorityQueue<int,int>(Comparer<int>.Create((a,b) => b.CompareTo(a)));
Avoid int priority negation at int.MinValue, and avoid b-a comparers because subtraction can overflow.

Use TryGetValue when you need both presence and the value:
if (d.TryGetValue(k, out var v)) { /* use v */ }
GetValueOrDefault(k, 0) is useful when absence should mean zero. ContainsKey followed by d[k] is valid, but repeats lookup; it is not forbidden.

Use StringBuilder for many incremental appends to a growing string:
var sb = new StringBuilder();
sb.Append(c);
return sb.ToString();
Repeated growing-string += can copy quadratically; a small fixed concatenation or interpolation is fine.

